General elections were held in [[Haiti]] on 28 November 2010, having originally been scheduled for 28 February. Ten [[Senate of Haiti|senators]] and all 99 [[Chamber of Deputies of Haiti|deputies]] were to be elected.<ref name="reuters">{{Cite news| url=https://www.reuters.com/article/worldNews/idUSTRE5AP03K20091126 | work=Reuters | title=Aristide party barred from Haiti's February ballot | date=2009-11-26}}</ref>

Presidential elections were also held. A run-off was originally scheduled for 16 January as no candidate received 50% of the votes cast.<ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.nytimes.com/2010/11/28/world/americas/28haiti.html?ref=americas | work=The New York Times | first=Randal C. | last=Archibold | title=Haiti Election, Set for Sunday, Marred by ID Card Problems | date=2010-11-27}}</ref> However, it was postponed until 20 March 2011 as election officials said they could not hold the runoff while awaiting results from re-polling,<ref name="english.people.com.cn">{{Cite web|title=Haitian ruling party's presidential candidate to quit electoral process - People's Daily Online|url=http://en.people.cn/90001/90777/90855/7273565.html|access-date=2021-02-09|website=en.people.cn|archive-date=2016-03-11|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160311073421/http://en.people.cn/90001/90777/90855/7273565.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> with results expected on 31 March.

Official results, announced on 21 April 2011, showed [[Michel Martelly]] defeating [[Mirlande Manigat]] in the second round of the presidential election.<ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-latin-america-13163550 | work=BBC News | title=Michel Martelly officially declared Haitian president | date=2011-04-21}}</ref><ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.nytimes.com/2011/04/05/world/americas/05haiti.html?hp | work=The New York Times | first=Randal C. | last=Archibold | title=Singer Michel Martelly Elected Haiti's President | date=2011-04-04}}</ref>

==Background==
Due to the [[2010 Haiti earthquake|January 2010 earthquake]], [[Haiti]]an presidential election was indefinitely postponed;<ref>{{Cite news| url=https://www.reuters.com/article/idUSN27162845._CH_.2400 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101202003531/http://www.reuters.com/article/idUSN27162845._CH_.2400 | url-status=dead | archive-date=2010-12-02 | work=Reuters | title=INTERVIEW-Haitian president says Feb. 28 elections postponed | first=Patricia | last=Zengerle | date=2010-01-27}}</ref> although November 28 was then decided as the date to hold the [[President of Haiti|presidential]] and [[National Assembly of Haiti|legislative]] [[Elections in Haiti|election]]s. Following the magnitude 7.0 earthquake, there were concerns of instability in the country, and the election came amid international pressure over instability in the country.<ref name="aljaz"/>  This would be the third democratic election in Haitian history.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|title=Haiti: Seismic Election|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/program/people-power/2010/11/18/haiti-seismic-election|access-date=2021-02-09|website=www.aljazeera.com|language=en}}</ref>

==Presidential candidates==
The deadline to file candidacy for the elections was August 7.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Daniel |first=Trenton |url=http://www.miamiherald.com/2010/08/08/1766529/at-least-20-candidates-file-to.html |title=At least 20 candidates file to run in Haiti's presidential elections - Haiti |publisher=MiamiHerald.com |date=2010-01-24 |access-date=2010-08-21}}</ref>

The list of presidential candidates was to have become official on August 17 after the nine-member provisional electoral council was to announce the eligibility criteria.<ref name="belpo">{{Cite web |url=http://www.belpolitik.com/haitianpolitics.php/25 |title=2010 Haiti Presidential Candidates List |publisher=Belpolitik.com |date=2006-12-15 |access-date=2010-08-21 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100818001936/http://www.belpolitik.com/haitianpolitics.php/25 |archive-date=2010-08-18 }}</ref> However, the election commission postponed its ruling until August 19 because of disagreement on the electoral law which stipulates that candidates must hold a Haitian passport and have five consecutive years of residence in Haiti, among other requirements. This was to affect [[Wyclef Jean]], [[Jacques-Édouard Alexis|Jacques Edouard Alexis]], and [[Leslie Voltaire]].<ref name="aljaz3">{{Cite web|url=http://english.aljazeera.net/news/americas/2010/08/2010818183714693940.html |title=Haiti presidential list delayed - Americas |publisher=Al Jazeera English |access-date=2010-08-21}}</ref>

The absence of the [[Fanmi Lavalas]] (FL) party was notable because of its popular support. [[Peter Hallward]] explained: "The final FL list of candidates was endorsed by the party leader ([[Jean Bertrand Aristide]]) by fax, but at the last minute the CEP invented a new requirement, knowing FL would be unable to meet it: Aristide, still exiled in South Africa and denied entry to Haiti, would have to sign the list in person."<ref>{{cite web |url=http://links.org.au/files/hallward-haiti-2010-exploiting-disaster-23-sept-2010.pdf |title=Haiti 2010: Exploiting Disaster 2010 - Haiti |access-date=2010-12-02 |archive-date=2020-07-22 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200722210614/http://links.org.au/files/hallward-haiti-2010-exploiting-disaster-23-sept-2010.pdf |url-status=dead }}</ref>

===Wyclef Jean===
Musician [[Wyclef Jean]], who left Haiti for the [[Haiti – United States relations|United States]] at the age of 9, said he is qualified to run for president and was in Haiti to initiate the legal process with lawyers and have his fingerprints taken by the judicial police to run for president. He did, however, declare that "There are a lot of rumors that I am running for president. I have not declared that. If we decide to move forward, I am pretty sure that we [would] have all our paperwork straight." He added that after discussions with his family he would "decide on what we're going to do because it is a big sacrifice." His aides said he would officially announce his candidacy on [[CNN]] in the United States before flying back to Haiti to enter the race. Some analysts predicted Jean's popularity with the youth of Haiti could help him "easily win the presidential election if his candidacy were approved."<ref>{{Cite news| url=https://www.reuters.com/article/idUSTRE66Q5TX20100730 | work=Reuters | title=Wyclef Jean steps toward Haitian presidential race | date=2010-07-30}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.usmagazine.com/moviestvmusic/news/wyclef-jean-to-run-for-president-of-haiti-2010267|title=Wyclef Jean to Run for President of Haiti?|publisher=UsMagazine.com}}</ref> On 5 August, he officially registered as a candidate for [[Viv Ansanm]] ("Live Together")<ref>{{cite news|title=Wyclef Jean files papers to stand for Haiti president|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/entertainment-arts-10888173|work=BBC News|access-date=6 August 2010 | date=2010-08-06}}</ref> party with the motto "''Fas a Fas''." ("Face to Face")<!--sourced TV--> The head of the party, [[Daniel Jean Jacques]], confirmed Jean would be the party's candidate for president. Jean spoke of his nomination as "a moment in time and in history. It's very emotional. The United States has Barack Obama and Haiti has Wyclef Jean."<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://english.aljazeera.net/news/americas/2010/08/20108522481897583.html |title=Hip-hop star vies for Haiti top job - Americas |publisher=Al Jazeera English |date=2010-08-06 |access-date=2010-08-21}}</ref> He told [[CNN]]'s [[Wolf Blitzer]] that he was running, despite Blitzer's questions about Jean's actual citizenship and passport facts. He also resigned from the chairmanship of ''[[Yéle Haiti]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.exclaim.ca/articles/generalarticlesynopsfullart.aspx?csid1=146&csid2=844&fid1=48631 |title=Exclaim! Magazine |access-date=2010-08-21 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120801084104/http://www.exclaim.ca/articles/generalarticlesynopsfullart.aspx?csid1=146&csid2=844&fid1=48631 |archive-date=2012-08-01 |url-status=dead }}</ref>
{{Wikinews|Wyclef Jean considering standing for president of Haiti}}
He was criticised by [[Pras Michel]], one of his former bandmates in the [[Fugees]], for the decision to run for president.<ref name="aljaz2">{{Cite web|url=http://english.aljazeera.net/news/americas/2010/08/20108843010173527.html |title=Ex-bandmate criticises Wyclef Jean - Americas |publisher=Al Jazeera English |date=2010-08-08 |access-date=2010-08-21}}</ref> Others criticised him for his lack of political experience and a vague platform he was to have run on. In the United States he was also criticised by [[Sean Penn]] and [[Arcade Fire]]'s [[Win Butler]] who said "Technically, [Wyclef Jean] shouldn't be eligible because he hasn't been a resident of Haiti. And I think him not speaking French and not being fluent in [[Creole language|Creole]] would be a really major issue in trying to run a really complex government, like the government in Haiti. It would kind of be like [[Arnold Schwarzenegger]] only speaking [[Austrian German|Austrian]] and being elected president of the United States after [[New York City]] and [[Los Angeles|L.A.]] had burned to the ground...I think he is a great musician and he really passionately cares about Haiti. I really hope he throws his support behind someone who is really competent and really eligible."<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://exclaim.ca/articles/generalarticlesynopsfullart.aspx?csid1=146&csid2=844&fid1=48988 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120708180713/http://exclaim.ca/articles/generalarticlesynopsfullart.aspx?csid1=146&csid2=844&fid1=48988 |url-status=dead |archive-date=2012-07-08 |title=Exclaim News: Arcade Fire's Win Butler Adds Opposition to Wyclef Jean's Haitian Presidential Plans |publisher=Exclaim.ca |date=2004-03-03 |access-date=2010-08-21 }}</ref>

On August 20, 2010, he was deemed ineligible to run for the presidency and his candidacy was rejected by Haiti's Electoral Council. While he accepted the ruling, many supporters protested the decision.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://english.aljazeera.net/news/americas/2010/08/2010821175952666775.html |title=Haitians rally for Jean's candidacy - Americas |publisher=Al Jazeera English |access-date=2010-08-21}}</ref> He asked his supporters to stay calm in the wake of the ruling. He also responded in saying he would file an appeal and that "[The political establishment] are trying to keep us out of the race." He argued that he could not comply with the law so strictly because President René Préval had appointed him as a roving ambassador in 2007 and he was allowed to travel and live outside the country.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Hip-hop star to appeal poll bid|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2010/8/23/hip-hop-star-to-appeal-poll-bid|access-date=2021-02-09|website=www.aljazeera.com|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.haitielections2010.com/details.php?id=360 |title=Haïti Elections 2010 :: Toutes les infos sur les élections haïtiennes de 2010<!-- Bot generated title --> |access-date=2010-08-23 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100920165004/http://haitielections2010.com/details.php?id=360 |archive-date=2010-09-20 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

===Final list of candidates===
There were supposed to have been 34 candidates in the preliminary race but a Haitian political website came up with 38.<ref name="belpo"/>

*[[Charles Henri Baker]], a businessman in the apparel industry. Charles Henri Baker is running under the [[Respect (Haiti)|Respè Party]].
*[[Jean-Henry Céant|Jean Henry Ceant]], a notaire and founder of Aimer Haiti which operated one of the few hospitals after the January 12 quake.
*[[Jacques-Édouard Alexis]], a two-time former prime minister<ref name="aljaz3"/> who was forced to resign in the aftermath of food riots in 2008.
*[[Jude Célestin]], executive director of the government's road-building outfit, the National Center of Equipment, and member of President René Préval's Unity (Inite) party.<ref>{{Cite news|last=|first=|date=2010-12-08|title=Factbox: Celestin technocrat protege of outgoing Haiti leader|language=en|work=Reuters|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-haiti-elections-celestin-idUSTRE6B70ND20101208|access-date=2021-02-09}}</ref>
*Eddy Delaleu, president, founder, and chief executive officer of the NGO ''Operation Hope for Children of Haiti'' since its inception in 1994.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://delaleuforhaiti2011.com/|title=Dr. Eddy Delaleu President for Haiti 2011|access-date=2010-08-21|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100214162219/http://delaleuforhaiti2011.com/|archive-date=2010-02-14}}</ref>
*Lavarice Gaudin, an Aristide ally and Miami activist and radio commentator.<ref>{{Cite news|date=2010-08-25|title=Wyclef Jean's disqualification signals Haiti diaspora not welcome in politics|work=Christian Science Monitor|url=https://www.csmonitor.com/World/Americas/2010/0825/Wyclef-Jean-s-disqualification-signals-Haiti-diaspora-not-welcome-in-politics|access-date=2021-02-09|issn=0882-7729}}</ref>
*[[Wilson Jeudi]], mayor of Delmas who organized a sister-city relationship with North Miami.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Haiti - Elections : Who is Wilson Jeudy ? - HaitiLibre.com : Haiti news 7/7|url=https://www.haitilibre.com/en/news-1240-haiti-elections-who-is-wilson-jeudy.html|access-date=2021-02-09|website=www.haitilibre.com}}</ref>
*[[Chavannes Jeune]], development agent, civil engineer and evangelist who came in 3rd place in 2006 election.
*[[Raymond Joseph]], former ambassador to the United States and Wyclef Jean's uncle.
*[[Mirlande Manigat]], a longtime opposition leader, professor, and former [[first lady]].<ref>{{Cite news|date=2010-12-08|title=Haiti election: Celestin and Manigat set for run-off|language=en-GB|work=BBC News|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-11945059|access-date=2021-02-09}}</ref>
*[[Michel Martelly]], a compas musician and entertainer whose lyrics have poked fun at the concept of the Haitian presidency.<ref>Wadner, Pierre (2010). [https://web.archive.org/web/20101225142350/http://www.dominionpaper.ca/weblogs/wadner_pierre/3799 "Michel Martelly, Stealth Duvalierist"] ''[[The Dominion (Canada)|The Dominion]]''. Dec 16, 2010. Retrieved Jan 24, 2011.</ref>
*[[Yvon Neptune]], an architect and ex-senator who served as prime minister under former President Jean-Bertrand Aristide.
*[[Leslie Voltaire]], a US-educated urban planner, former minister,<ref name="aljaz3"/> and government liaison to the United Nations.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Haiti - Elections : Who is Leslie Voltaire? - HaitiLibre.com : Haiti news 7/7|url=https://www.haitilibre.com/en/news-1191-haiti-elections-who-is-leslie-voltaire.html|access-date=2021-02-09|website=www.haitilibre.com}}</ref>

Other candidates included Axan Abellard, [[Charles Voigt]], Claire Lydie Parent, [[Déjean Bélizaire]], Duroseau Vilaire Cluny, Eric Charles, Francois Turnier, Garaudy Laguerre, Gary Guiteau, Genard Joseph, Gerard Blot, Guy Theodore, Jacques Philippe Eugene, [[Jean Bertin]], Jean Hector Anacacis, Josette Bijou, Kesnel Dalmacy, Leon Jeune, Mario Eddy Rodriguez, Menelas Vilsaint, Olicier Pieriche, Rene Saint-Fort, Wilkens C. Gilles, [[Yves Cristalin]], and Paul Arthur Fleurival.

==Campaign==
President René Préval told the UN, "It is important that we take this difficult process to a conclusion, with rigour, equality, and transparency, essential conditions for consolidating our young democracy. I therefore call on all the national stakeholders and our overseas friends so we can successfully cross this electoral crossroads together."<ref>https://gadebate.un.org/Portals/1/statements/634209430444531250HT_fr.pdf{{Dead link|date=August 2019 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}<nowiki/>{{Dead link|date=February 2021}}</ref>

Two issues for candidates to answer were considered rebuilding from the earthquake earlier in the year and an [[2010 Haiti cholera outbreak|outbreak of cholera]] less than two months before the election, which was blamed on the United Nations by many Haitians.<ref name=":0" /> Independent research confirmed the origin of the cholera outbreak as being linked to UN Nepalese troops, and a 2011 UN Report admitted as much. The UN alleged that the outbreak was used for "political reasons because of forthcoming elections," as the government in Haiti sent its own forces to "protect" the peacekeepers.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Haiti protests against UN|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2010/11/17/haiti-riots-against-un-heat-up|access-date=2021-02-09|website=www.aljazeera.com|language=en}}</ref>

The election was termed in the media as a "seismic" one.<ref name=":0" />

