==Conduct==
On the day of the first round of voting, Martelly and Manigat, as well as most other candidates, denounced the results as invalid by noon, before voting had even concluded.  A notable exception to this denunciation was Jude Celestin.

Following demonstration and demands by the opposition to hold elections amid a parliamentary call that he can stay beyond his 7 February mandate, President [[René Préval]] issued decrees ordering the country's nine-member provisional electoral council to organise the election. However he still did not address calls for the council itself to be replaced before a vote is held.<ref name="aljaz">{{Cite web|url=http://english.aljazeera.net/news/americas/2010/06/201062821156633689.html |title=November polls for Haiti - Americas |publisher=Al Jazeera English |date=2010-06-28 |access-date=2010-08-21}}</ref> Despite the constitutional legitimacy and impartiality of the [[Provisional Electoral Council|CEP]] having been challenged.<ref name="IJDH">{{Cite web |url=http://ijdh.org/archives/13138 |title=The International Community Should pressure the Haitian Government For Prompt and Fair Elections |publisher=Institute for Justice and Democracy in Haiti |date=2010-06-30 |access-date=2011-01-08 |archive-date=2010-11-20 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101120063342/http://ijdh.org/archives/13138 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Some questioned whether Haiti was ready to hold an election following the earthquake that left more than a million people in makeshift camps and without IDs. There was also a fear that the election could throw the country into a political crisis due to a lack of transparency and voting fraud.<ref name="al jaz">{{Cite web|url=http://english.aljazeera.net/news/americas/2010/08/201088152649176110.html |title=Haiti presidential poll concerns - Americas |publisher=Al Jazeera English |date=2010-08-08 |access-date=2010-08-21}}</ref>

[[Foreign relations of Canada|Canada]] had also pressured Haiti to hold elections as stipulated in its [[Constitution of Haiti|constitution]]. It wanted a firm commitment to holding elections by the end of the year as domestic opposition grew to incumbent President René Préval's response to the earthquake.<ref>{{cite news|author=The Canadian Press |url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/montreal/canada-presses-for-haitian-elections-1.956254#ixzz0w3iOKgnL |title=CBC News - Montreal - Canada presses for Haitian elections |publisher=Cbc.ca |date=2010-05-21 |access-date=2010-08-21}}</ref>

Due to the elections and allegedly accepted instability, Haitian security services were given special training by foreign forces.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Haiti election security|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2010/10/11/haiti-security-tight-ahead-of-poll|access-date=2021-02-09|website=www.aljazeera.com|language=en}}</ref>

The [[United Nations]] voted to extend [[MINUSTAH]]'s mandate amid fears of instability. This was greeted with protesters in [[Port-au-Prince]] carrying banners reading "Down with the occupation," and burning the [[flag of Brazil]], as they had the largest contingent in MINUSTAH. Despite months of relative calm after the earthquake, this represented a "chorus of opposition to René Préval, Haiti's president, and to UN forces."<ref>{{Cite web|title=Shots fired into air at Haiti protest|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2010/10/15/anti-un-protesters-block-haiti-base|access-date=2021-02-09|website=www.aljazeera.com|language=en}}</ref>

Due to the cholera outbreak, there were fears that the election could be postponed. However, the head of MINUSTAH, [[Edmond Mulet]], said that it should not be delayed as that could lead to a political vacuum with untold potential problems.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Unrest 'must not stop Haiti polls'|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2010/11/20/unrest-must-not-stop-haiti-polls|access-date=2021-02-09|website=www.aljazeera.com|language=en}}</ref>

After a visit by then Secretary of State Hillary Clinton, Manigat and Martelly agreed to accept the results of the first round of voting.

During the first round of voting, two people were killed in a firefight between rival parties' supporters in [[Aquin]], with several injured in violence across the country.<ref name=":1" />

===Post-1st round===
There were calls for Martelly to be included in the run-off; however, he has stated that he would not take part if Célestin is also a candidate in the run-off.<ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-latin-america-11950240 | work=BBC News | title=Haiti ruling party HQ set alight by election protesters | date=2010-12-08}}</ref> A recount was announced on 10 December.<ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-latin-america-11964180 | work=BBC News | title=Haiti orders recount of disputed presidential election | date=2010-12-10}}</ref> However, both Manigat and Martelly then rejected the proposed recount, with only Célestin accepting it. Manigat and Martelly also criticised the lack of clear procedures or a timetable.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Candidates reject recount in sloppy Haiti election|url=https://apnews.com/article/61d22e48698542cc9342cb191fba788b|access-date=2021-02-09|website=AP NEWS|date=11 December 2010 }}</ref>

Following a deal reached in late December 2010 to look into the vote counting process and have re-polling in certain constituencies,{{Which|date=January 2011}} the run-off was postponed until at least February 2011.<ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.nytimes.com/2010/12/31/opinion/31fri2.html | work=The New York Times | title=Haiti's Presidential Election | date=2010-12-30}}</ref> Pierre-Louis Opont, the director general of the Provisional Electoral Council said: "It will be materially impossible to hold the run-off on January 16. From the date of the publication of the final results of the first round, we will need at least one month to hold the run-off." He also said that a report by the [[Organisation of American States]] and a completion of review for the results that had been challenged would be needed first.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Delva|first=Joseph Guyler|date=2011-01-04|title=Haiti second round vote impossible before February|language=en|work=Reuters|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/cnews-us-haiti-elections-idCATRE7034TP20110104|access-date=2021-02-09}}</ref>

The OAS report proposed that Manigat and Martelly advance to the run-off, dropping Célestin from the second round.<ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-latin-america-12158139 | work=BBC News | title=OAS to give Haiti presidential election verdict | date=2011-01-11}}</ref> However, the Provisional Electoral Council rejected that suggestion on 19 January 2011.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Haiti's electoral body rejects OAS recount result - People's Daily Online|url=http://en.people.cn/90001/90777/90852/7267259.html|access-date=2021-02-09|website=en.people.cn}}</ref> Additionally, some analyses showed that the OAS report was statistically flawed and made the recommendation to eliminate Célestin with no apparent justification.  In a January 2011 report, the [[Center for Economic and Policy Research]] concluded that 71.5% of the eligible Haitian population did not vote during the first round of the election on November 28, 2010.<ref name="CEPR">[http://www.cepr.net/documents/publications/haiti-2011-01.pdf Haiti's Fatally Flawed Election] CEPR, January 1, 2011</ref> Furthermore, from the remaining votes 8.1% were considered to be invalid, leaving only 20.1% of the votes being cast on one of the candidates in the election. The high rate of irregular votes led the CEPR to conclude that no statistics-based, non-arbitrary selection of a second-place candidate was possible.<ref name="CEPR"/> In a later August 2011 report, the CEPR further concluded that the data actually contradicted the OAS recommendation to overturn the initial vote counts and select Martelly as the second-place candidate, and that this move was not in line with normal international election resolution processes, suggesting a bias on the part of the OAS.<ref>[http://cepr.net/documents/publications/haiti-oas-2011-10.pdf The Organization of American States in Haiti: Election Monitoring or Political Intervention?] CEPR, August 2011</ref>

Célestin was reportedly likely to drop out of the run-off by himself, however, as stated by a member of his party on 25 January 2011, partially as a result of international pressure.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web|date=2011-01-25|title=Célestin to withdraw from Haiti elections|url=https://www.rfi.fr/en/americas/20110125-celestin-withdraw-haiti-elections|access-date=2021-02-09|website=RFI|language=en}}</ref> The official withdrawal statement came on 26 January 2011.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Haitian ruling party's presidential candidate withdraws from elections - People's Daily Online|url=http://en.people.cn/90001/90777/90852/7274183.html|access-date=2021-02-09|website=en.people.cn}}</ref>

[[Jean Claude Duvalier|Baby Doc]] returned to Haiti on 16 January 2011 in what was seen as possibly more uncertainty.<ref name=":3">{{Cite news|date=2011-01-17|title='Baby Doc' Duvalier returns to Haiti from exile|language=en-GB|work=BBC News|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-11943820|access-date=2021-02-09}}</ref> Corruption and theft charges were then filed against him.<ref name=":3" /> Jean-Bertrand Aristide returned in March amidst concern for stability by both the runoff candidates.<ref>{{Cite news|date=2011-03-18|title=Haiti ex-leader Jean-Bertrand Aristide back after exile|language=en-GB|work=BBC News|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-12777005|access-date=2021-02-09}}</ref> The rumour of his return led to several thousand of his supporters in [[Port-au-Prince]] demanding that without Aristide there should not be a second round election.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Aristide backers march amid talk of Haiti return – HAITIAN-TRUTH.ORG Proud to be Haiti's most informative NEWS site|url=https://www.haitian-truth.org/aristide-backers-march-amid-talk-of-haiti-return/|access-date=2021-02-09|language=en-US}}</ref> The protest even reached as far as Miami, where approximately 60 protesters gathered in front of the offices of ''[[The Miami Herald]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.radiotelevisioncaraibes.com/nouvelles/nouvellesdiaspora/3426.html |title = Aristide supporters protest outside The Miami Herald - Radio Television Caraibes  |access-date=2011-03-30 |archive-date=2011-03-21 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110321092800/http://www.radiotelevisioncaraibes.com/nouvelles/nouvellesdiaspora/3426.html |url-status=usurped }}</ref> Questions were then asked about the effect the former presidents could have on the political process.

Campaigning for the second round of the presidential election officially commenced on Thursday, 17 February. While [[Mirlande Manigat]] discussed her future plans for Haiti in a hotel with reporters, Martelly took to the streets of [[Cap-Haïtien]], the second largest city in Haiti, where he and his followers danced and sang in the streets.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Campaign opens for Haiti's presidential runoff|url=https://canada-haiti.ca/content/campaign-opens-haiti%E2%80%99s-presidential-runoff|access-date=2021-02-09|website=Campaign opens for Haiti’s presidential runoff|language=en}}</ref> Martelly even went as far as to campaign in [[Miami]] in the [[United States of America]],<ref>{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=2011-02-24|title=Haitian candidate 'Sweet Micky' campaigns in Fla.|url=https://www.realclearpolitics.com/news/ap/politics/2011/Feb/24/haitian_candidate__sweet_micky__campaigns_in_fla_.html|access-date=2021-02-09|website=www.realclearpolitics.com}}</ref> which is home to a large number of Haitian [[expatriate]]s.

On 17 February, Wyclef Jean announced his support for Martelly in the second round of the presidential election.<ref>{{Cite news|title=Wyclef Jean backs musician in Haiti election|language=en|work=Reuters|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-haiti-wyclef-idUSTRE71G0BD20110217|access-date=2021-02-09}}</ref> Wyclef Jean was also shot at and grazed by a bullet the day before the second round vote during campaigning for Martelly. He was wounded and taken to a hospital.<ref name=":1" />

During voting for the second round some polling stations failed to open on time as they lacked the necessary equipment and documents.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Voting opens in cholera-hit Haiti|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2010/11/28/voting-opens-in-cholera-hit-haiti|access-date=2021-02-09|website=www.aljazeera.com|language=en}}</ref>

President [[Barack Obama]] of the US asked the [[government of South Africa]] to delay former president Jean-Bertrand Aristide's attempt to return to Haiti,<ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.nytimes.com/2011/03/15/world/americas/15briefs-ART-Haiti.html?_r=1 | work=The New York Times | title=Haiti: U.S. Asks South Africa to Delay Aristide's Departure | date=2011-03-14}}</ref> however, the request was rejected by Aristide himself.<ref>{{cite web|last=|first=|date=2011-03-15|title=President Jean Bertrand Aristide rejects U.S. request to "postpone his return"in Haiti|url=http://www.ayitinou.com/article-posts/29-haitian-articles/2967-president-jean-bertrand-aristide-rejects-us-request-to-qpostpone-his-returnqin-haiti.html|url-status=usurped|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110320050555/http://ayitinou.com/article-posts/29-haitian-articles/2967-president-jean-bertrand-aristide-rejects-us-request-to-qpostpone-his-returnqin-haiti.html|archive-date=2011-03-20|access-date=2011-03-30|website=}}</ref>

